{
  "requirement": "Borrow the identity of the source node being checked.\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<'d, D: Domain> ValidationContext<'d, D> {\n    pub fn node_id(&self) -> &NodeId<D>;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject `requirements.sdk.graph.validation-context.ValidationContext.node_id` has one obligation. `node_id(&self) -> &NodeId<D>` must borrow the identity of the node being checked, meaning the node the context was constructed for.\n\n- **Behavior:** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[labeled_selections_track_only_target_membership_without_reading_contents]` is marked `{test}` for this requirement. It builds `ValidationContext::new(&graph, graph.cache().intern_node(1))` and asserts `ctx.node_id() == &graph.cache().intern_node(1)`. That checks the returned identity against the constructed node. The same test also queries other nodes through `outgoing_edges`, using node 9 and later node 1 again. Those queries are for other requirements, but they show the context identity is not simply the queried node.\n- **Borrowed return type:** the reference-returning signature is enforced at compile time. `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` also compiles the downstream `habeas` consumer against the SDK. That test is not specific to this method and adds little here.\n- **Gaps:** I found none. The accessor is trivial, and the only way to get it wrong is to return a different node. The assertion would catch that. I did not request implementation source.",
